    "done_when": "reckon crew velocity in reckon/cli.py serves the same velocity view as crew(view=\"velocity\"). It follows the crew ledger command's pattern and options, and takes --since, --until, --project (one project, or * for every mounted one), --fields, --limit and --cursor, with the same meanings and refusals as the MCP view.\n\nBoth surfaces call one function in reckon/velocity.py that owns the window parsing, the mount resolution, the default summary, and the paging of the project-by-lane-by-day cells. Whatever of that composition lives only in reckon/mcp.py today moves into velocity.py, and mcp.py keeps a thin call, so no derivation exists twice.\n\ntests/test_crew_velocity_cli.py synthesises its repositories under tmp_path, as tests/test_crew_velocity_view.py does, and reads nothing outside tmp_path. It asserts:\n- the CLI's JSON output equals the MCP view's for the same window and fields, a parity case over one default call and one cells page;\n- the CLI refuses a missing --since by name.\n\nThat file, tests/test_crew_velocity_view.py and tests/test_velocity.py pass at exit 0. The manifest writes baseline_suite and after_suite each as one line of armed JSON: revision, command with no placeholder, exit_status, completed, failure_count, failure_ids, log_path. The head arm runs at the committed head, and every gate log opens with one line naming its revision, tree and command. The declared negative control is applied and observed to fail, and its log's absolute path is in the manifest with an EXIT= line and the FAILED id. The full project suite is not run by this node.",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "Serve the velocity view as reckon crew velocity through the same composition function the MCP view uses, so the two surfaces return the same payload for the same window",
